\begin{document}
\setcounter{section}{7}
\section*{HKO: revised strategy and feasible-section construction}
The HKO example is already known to violate Viterbo's predicted bound. The
local question is whether the same ten-facet model contains a nearby
improvement, or whether the HKO point is locally best once translations,
scalings, and linear symplectic changes of coordinates have been quotiented
out. This section proves the latter statement by reducing the geometric local
problem to a finite exact certificate.

Let \(\mathcal K\) be the space of convex bodies in \(\mathbb R^4\), equipped
with the Hausdorff metric, and let \(\mathcal P_{10}\subset\mathcal K\) be the
subspace of convex polytopes with exactly ten facets. Let
\(P_5\subset\mathbb R^2\) be the centered regular pentagon of circumradius
\(1\) with a vertex at
\((1,0)\), and let \(R(\theta)\) denote counterclockwise rotation. \[
  K_{\mathrm{HKO}}=P_5\times_L R(-\pi/2)P_5.
\]
\begin{theorem}[HKO is locally maximal in the ten-facet space]
\label{thm:hko-ten-facet-local-maximum}
There is a Hausdorff neighborhood \(\mathcal U\) of \(K_{\mathrm{HKO}}\) in
\(\mathcal P_{10}\) such that
\[
  \operatorname{sys}(K)\leq \operatorname{sys}(K_{\mathrm{HKO}})
  \qquad (K\in\mathcal U).
\]
Moreover, after shrinking \(\mathcal U\) if necessary, the equality set is
exactly the part of the \(G_{\operatorname{sys}}\)-orbit of
\(K_{\mathrm{HKO}}\) that remains in \(\mathcal U\):
\[
  \{K\in\mathcal U:
    \operatorname{sys}(K)=\operatorname{sys}(K_{\mathrm{HKO}})\}
  =
  \mathcal U\cap G_{\operatorname{sys}}\cdot K_{\mathrm{HKO}}.
\]
\end{theorem}

The equality clause is necessary because translations, positive scalings
and linear symplectic maps preserve the ratio.
After removing these symmetries, every sufficiently small ten-facet
perturbation strictly decreases the ratio. The theorem leaves open both
improvements farther away and nearby improvements that change the number of
facets.

\paragraph{How touching upper bounds force a maximum.}
After removing the symmetry directions, we construct several smooth upper
bounds for the systolic ratio near the HKO body. Each agrees with the ratio
at that body. Together they cover every nonzero remaining direction: in each
direction, at least one bound has strictly negative slope. We show that this
forces the ratio to decrease throughout some sufficiently small punctured
neighborhood in the transverse slice. No explicit neighborhood size is
needed for the theorem.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.35cm,y=0.9cm]
    \fill[black!8] (-1.8,-0.35) -- (-1.8,0.2) -- (0,2)
      -- (1.8,0.2) -- (1.8,-0.35) -- cycle;
    \draw[->,black!55] (-2.05,0) -- (2.1,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->,black!55] (0,-0.45) -- (0,4.1);
    \node[below left] at (0,0) {$0$};
    \draw[dashed,thick] (-1.8,3.8) -- (0,2);
    \draw[dashed,thick] (0,2) -- (1.8,3.8);
    \draw[very thick] (-1.8,0.2) -- (0,2) -- (1.8,0.2);
    \fill (0,2) circle (1.8pt);
    \node[anchor=east,fill=white,inner sep=2pt] at (-0.07,2) {$s_0$};
    \node[above] at (-1.8,3.95) {$s_0-x$};
    \node[above] at (1.8,3.95) {$s_0+x$};
    \node[anchor=west,fill=white,inner sep=2pt] at (0.9,1.25)
      {$s_0-|x|$};
  \end{tikzpicture}
  \caption{Two touching upper bounds in one variable. Suppose
  \(f(0)=s_0\), \(f(x)\leq s_0+x\), and \(f(x)\leq s_0-x\).
  For \(x>0\) the second bound decreases; for \(x<0\) the first does.
  Thus \(f(x)\leq s_0-|x|<f(0)\) whenever \(x\ne0\).
  The bold line is the lower envelope of the two bounds, not the graph of
  \(f\); the shaded region lies below both bounds.}
  \label{fig:hko-touching-upper-bounds}
\end{figure}


We obtain these bounds from smooth feasible choices of Haim--Kislev weights,
as in Lemma~\ref{lem:first-order-feasible-section-upper-function}. In the
row coordinates \(a\) of \eqref{eq:first-order-row-chart}, each such choice
gives a positive action \(A_\sigma(a)\) with
\(c_{\mathrm{EHZ}}(K(a))\leq A_\sigma(a)\). Squaring and dividing by twice
the volume gives
\[
  \operatorname{sys}(K(a))\leq
  U_\sigma(a)=\frac{A_\sigma(a)^2}{2\operatorname{vol}_4(K(a))}.
\]
Our certificate uses a selection of \(26\) such functions, all touching the
systolic ratio at HKO. What matters about this selection is that its
derivatives cover the transverse directions in the sense just described.

To compare their slopes, we first need coordinates for all nearby bodies.
The next subsection identifies a Hausdorff-local ten-facet neighborhood with
a neighborhood of the ten labelled polar vertices
\(a_{\mathrm{HKO}}\in(\mathbb R^4)^{10}\). The symmetry orbit has tangent
dimension \(15\); a transverse slice \(a_{\mathrm{HKO}}+S\) therefore has
dimension \(25\). Locally, every nearby body is a symmetry transform of a
point on this slice, so strict decrease there proves the theorem, including
its equality clause.

For our selected functions, the certificate verifies that the derivative rows
\(r_\sigma=DU_\sigma(a_{\mathrm{HKO}})\) have restrictions to \(S\) that
span \(S^*\), and satisfy
\[
  \sum_{\sigma\in\Sigma}\lambda_\sigma r_\sigma=0,
  \qquad \lambda_\sigma>0,
  \qquad \sum_{\sigma\in\Sigma}\lambda_\sigma=1,
  \qquad |\Sigma|=26.
\]
These are the hypotheses of
Lemma~\ref{lem:first-order-finite-upper-function-criterion}. In every nonzero
transverse direction, at least one touching upper function has negative
derivative. The lemma's uniform Taylor argument then gives strict decrease
throughout a sufficiently small punctured neighborhood in the slice.

A search produces a candidate finite witness; exact computation checks its
algebraic equations, inequalities and ranks. The chart, feasible-section and
slice arguments explain why the checked properties imply the geometric
theorem.

\subsection*{Coordinate definitions from Section 6}
Fix a labelled presentation
\begin{equation}\label{eq:first-order-row-chart}
  K(a)=\{x\in\mathbb R^4:
    \langle a_i,x\rangle\leq 1,\ i=1,\ldots,F\},
  \qquad
  a=(a_1,\ldots,a_F).
\end{equation}
\(V(a)=\operatorname{vol}_4(K(a))\).
\begin{equation}\label{eq:first-order-fixed-word-q}
  Q_\sigma(a,\beta)
  =
  \sum_{1\leq j<k\leq m}
    \beta_j\beta_k\,
    \omega_0(a_{\sigma_j},a_{\sigma_k}).
\end{equation}
\subsection{Exact finite certificate}
\label{subsec:hko-local-maximum-computation-with-sagemath}

The certificate constructs \(26\) smooth upper functions touching the systolic
ratio at \(a_0=a_{\mathrm{HKO}}\). Their derivatives will satisfy
Lemma~\ref{lem:first-order-finite-upper-function-criterion} on the transverse
slice. We first give the functions explicitly, then state the finite equations
used to check their derivatives.

Fix a word \(\sigma=(\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_m)\) of distinct facets. As in
Section~\ref{subsec:first-order-hk-branches}, let \(C_\sigma(a)\) be the
\(5\times m\) matrix whose \(k\)-th column is
\((a_{\sigma_k},1)\), and put \(e=(0,0,0,0,1)^{\mathsf T}\).
Thus \(C_\sigma(a)\beta=e\) consists of four closure equations and
\(\sum_k\beta_k=1\). For this word, the finite data are a vector
\(\beta^0>0\) and a set \(I\) of five column indices such that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:hko-section-base-checks}
\begin{gathered}
  C_\sigma(a_0)\beta^0=e,\qquad
  \det C_{\sigma,I}(a_0)\ne0,\\
  Q_\sigma(a_0,\beta^0)=\frac{1}{2c_{\mathrm{EHZ}}(K_{\mathrm{HKO}})}.
\end{gathered}
\end{equation}
Here \(Q_\sigma\) is the quadratic form
\eqref{eq:first-order-fixed-word-q}. Let \(J\) be the complementary column
indices and set \(b=\beta^0_J\). Holding these \(m-5\) weights fixed leaves
five linear equations for the other five weights:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:hko-explicit-feasible-section}
  \beta_{\sigma,J}(a)=b,\qquad
  \beta_{\sigma,I}(a)
    =C_{\sigma,I}(a)^{-1}\bigl(e-C_{\sigma,J}(a)b\bigr).
\end{equation}
The determinant stays nonzero near \(a_0\), so this formula is smooth.
Substitution gives \(C_\sigma(a)\beta_\sigma(a)=e\); uniqueness of the
five-variable solve gives \(\beta_\sigma(a_0)=\beta^0\). Consequently all
weights remain positive in a sufficiently small neighborhood. In that
neighborhood, shrinking it again if necessary, put
\begin{equation}\label{eq:hko-section-upper-function}
 \begin{aligned}
  q_\sigma(a)&=Q_\sigma(a,\beta_\sigma(a))>0,\qquad
  A_\sigma(a)=\frac{1}{2q_\sigma(a)},\\
  U_\sigma(a)&=\frac{A_\sigma(a)^2}{2V(a)}
             =\frac{1}{8V(a)q_\sigma(a)^2}.
 \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Lemma~\ref{lem:first-order-feasible-section-upper-function} gives
\(\operatorname{sys}(K(a))\leq U_\sigma(a)\), with equality at \(a_0\)
by \eqref{eq:hko-section-base-checks}.

The selected words have length six or seven, so the construction holds one
or two weights fixed and solves for the remaining five. Its local extension
requires the checked closure/\hspace{0pt}normalization minor to be invertible, rather
than regularity of a KKT system or a choice of nearby optimizing branches.


